= Ironic process theory — Technical =

== Dual-process architecture ==
The model can be summarized as:

<pre>
control goal: avoid state X

operating process:
search for not-X / desired state
resource demanding

monitoring process:
search for evidence of X
relatively less demanding
</pre>

The monitoring process must keep X diagnostically accessible enough to detect control failure.

== Ironic accessibility ==
If operating control is weakened:

<pre>
monitor(X) remains active
operate(not-X) weakens
→ relative accessibility(X) may rise
</pre>

This yields predictions for cognitive load, time pressure, distraction, and termination of active control.

== Relation to dual-process theories ==
The operating/monitoring distinction is specific to Wegner's mental-control architecture.

It should not be collapsed into generic System 1/System 2 frameworks.

== Empirical status ==
Evidence includes thought-accessibility, rebound, motor-control, mood-control, and performance paradigms.

Effect size and robustness vary with method. Recent reviews of thought suppression caution against treating rebound as inevitable.

== Falsifiability ==
The theory is strongest where it generates differential predictions, such as increased target accessibility under load relative to low-load control conditions.

Post hoc labeling of any control failure as “ironic” is weaker evidence.

